steps.body:
To merge `k` sorted lists into one, you keep taking the smallest value still at the front of any list. Checking every front costs O(k) per value. A heap holds one front per list instead and hands over the smallest in O(log k).

Each entry is an `int[]` of three slots, `{value, list, index}`: the value, which list it came from, and where it sits in that list. The comparator orders by value and breaks ties by list number, so the order is fixed. After you poll an entry, offer the next value from the same list, if it has one:

```java
int[][] lists = {{1, 4, 7}, {2, 5}, {}, {0, 6, 9}};
PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> {
    if (a[0] != b[0]) {
        return Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]);
    }
    return Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]);
});
for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {
    if (lists[i].length > 0) {
        heap.offer(new int[]{lists[i][0], i, 0});
    }
}
List<Integer> merged = new ArrayList<>();
while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    int[] e = heap.poll();
    merged.add(e[0]);
    int next = e[2] + 1;
    if (next < lists[e[1]].length) {
        heap.offer(new int[]{lists[e[1]][next], e[1], next});
    }
}
System.out.println(merged); // [0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9]
```

An empty list has no front, so it never gets an entry. The heap never holds more than `k` entries, one per list, so merging `N` values in all costs O(N log k) time and O(k) extra space, where copying everything into one array and sorting costs O(N log N). The heap also lets you stop early, after the first few values.

